{"content":"Found and fixed the regression: in `Token List.token_first()` (sqlparse/sql.py), the inner `matcher` function had inverted logic — it returned `True` for whitespace/comment tokens, causing `_token_matching` (which returns the first token where `func()` is `True`) to return the first whitespace/comment token instead of skipping it. This made `get_type()` see a whitespace token as the "first" token, so it never matched `T.Keyword.DML`/`DDL`, always falling through to 'UNKNOWN'.

Fixed by negating the condition (matching the same pattern already used correctly in `token_next`'s matcher): now matcher returns `True` for tokens that should be *kept* (i.e., NOT whitespace/comment to skip), so `token_first` correctly returns the first non-whitespace (and optionally non-comment) token.

Now running the test suite to confirm the fix and that nothing else broke.","tool_calls":[{"id":"verify_1","name":"run","arguments":{"command":"python -c "import sqlparse; print(sqlparse.parse('SELECT 1')[0].get_type())""}},{"id":"verify_2","name":"run","arguments":{"command":"pytest tests -q","timeout_seconds":120}}]}

